 QuickNotes Data Model

 1. Database choice

QuickNotes will use PostgreSQL, a relational SQL database.

Notes, users, tags, and note-tag associations have clear relationships.
The application needs foreign-key constraints, transactions, reliable
updates, unique email addresses, and flexible querying.

PostgreSQL supports these requirements and can scale through indexing,
read replicas, connection pooling, and vertical or horizontal
architectural improvements.

 2. Entities and columns

 Users

| Column | Type | Key / constraint |
|---|---|---|
| id | BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY | Primary key |
| name | VARCHAR(100) | NOT NULL |
| email | VARCHAR(255) | NOT NULL, UNIQUE |
| password_hash | VARCHAR(255) | NOT NULL |
| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | NOT NULL |
| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | NOT NULL |

 Notes

| Column | Type | Key / constraint |
|---|---|---|
| id | BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY | Primary key |
| user_id | BIGINT | Foreign key to users.id |
| title | VARCHAR(100) | NOT NULL |
| body | TEXT | NOT NULL, default empty string |
| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | NOT NULL |
| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | NOT NULL |

 Tags

| Column | Type | Key / constraint |
|---|---|---|
| id | BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY | Primary key |
| user_id | BIGINT | Foreign key to users.id |
| name | VARCHAR(50) | NOT NULL |
| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | NOT NULL |

A user cannot create duplicate tag names within their own account.

 Note_tags

| Column | Type | Key / constraint |
|---|---|---|
| note_id | BIGINT | Primary key component; FK to notes.id |
| tag_id | BIGINT | Primary key component; FK to tags.id |
| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | NOT NULL |

The composite primary key `(note_id, tag_id)` prevents the same tag
from being attached to the same note more than once.
  
 3. Relationships

 One-to-many: users and notes

One user can own many notes, but each note belongs to exactly one user.

`users.id -> notes.user_id`
  
 One-to-many: users and tags

One user can create many tags, but each tag belongs to one user.

`users.id -> tags.user_id`

Many-to-many: notes and tags

A note can have multiple tags, and a tag can be associated with
multiple notes.

The `note_tags` junction table implements this relationship:

`notes.id -> note_tags.note_id`

`tags.id -> note_tags.tag_id`

The application must verify that a note and tag belong to the same
user before creating an association.

## 4. CREATE TABLE statements

```sql
CREATE TABLE users (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    email VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE,
    password_hash VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

CREATE TABLE notes (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    user_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id)
        ON DELETE CASCADE,
    title VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL
        CHECK (length(trim(title)) > 0),
    body TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '',
    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

CREATE TABLE tags (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    user_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id)
        ON DELETE CASCADE,
    name VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL
        CHECK (length(trim(name)) > 0),
    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    CONSTRAINT unique_user_tag_name UNIQUE (user_id, name),
    CONSTRAINT unique_tag_owner_pair UNIQUE (user_id, id)
);

CREATE TABLE note_tags (
    note_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES notes(id)
        ON DELETE CASCADE,
    tag_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES tags(id)
        ON DELETE CASCADE,
    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    PRIMARY KEY (note_id, tag_id)
);
